<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · 1895 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">Waltzing Matilda</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A bush ballad written by Banjo Paterson in outback Queensland in 1895 that many call Australia's unofficial anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Andrew Barton &quot;Banjo&quot; Paterson (words altered by Harry Nathan, 1902, and Marie Cowan, 1903)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Christina Macpherson, from Thomas Bulch's march &quot;Craigielee&quot; after James Barr's setting of &quot;Thou Bonnie Wood o Craigielee&quot;; arranged by Harry Nathan (1902) and Marie Cowan (1903)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Australian bush ballad tradition; the pastoral workers of outback Queensland</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (Australian)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Winton and Dagworth Station, Queensland, Australia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1895 (words written August-September 1895; first published setting 20 December 1902; Cowan's version 1903)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Banjo Paterson (1895; he died in 1941); the best-known version by Marie Cowan, 1903)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>In 1895 Andrew Barton Paterson, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> solicitor who published verse as &quot;The Banjo&quot;, travelled to Winton in central-western Queensland to visit his fiancée Sarah Riley. At a gathering there Christina Macpherson, a school friend of Sarah's, played a tune that caught his attention, and he wrote a first verse to it on the spot. She had heard it at the Warrnambool races in Victoria on 24 April 1894: the march &quot;Craigielee&quot; by the English-born Australian Thomas Bulch, whose first strain came from James Barr's 1818 setting of &quot;Thou Bonnie Wood o Craigielee&quot; by the Paisley weaver-poet Robert Tannahill. Christina picked it out by ear on a piano, imperfectly, and in doing so gave it the AABA form in which it is still sung; at her family's Dagworth Station, which had no piano, she played it on an autoharp. The rest of the song was written over three or four weeks in August and early September 1895, at Dagworth and nearby, and was sung at Winton and at Oondooroo Station, where Herbert Ramsay performed it dressed as a swagman. Paterson's notebook draft and Christina's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> survive in the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p>The words tell of a swagman, an itinerant worker, who takes a sheep and dies in a billabong rather than be caught by the squatter and the troopers. The year before, during the renewed shearers' strike of 1894, striking shearers had burned the woolshed at Dagworth on 2 September, and the same day the union leader Samuel Hoffmeister was found shot dead at a billabong near Kynuna, a death the inquest ruled suicide and that has been questioned since. Historians such as Ross Fitzgerald see the strike behind the song; others, among them Peter and Sheila Forrest in 2008, argue that Paterson wrote it as part of a flirtation with Christina Macpherson and that the strike reading is a later political appropriation. Claims that Paterson reworked an older bush song have no evidence behind them, and a supposed source in an Irish jig shows no resemblance. A belief that the song was first sung on 6 April 1895 is wrong.</p><p>The song spread slowly at first. Harry Nathan published the first setting, with altered words and a new variation of the tune, on 20 December 1902. On 12 January 1903 Paterson sold the rights, with other pieces, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a> &amp; Robertson for five pounds; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> merchant James Inglis bought them soon after and asked Marie Cowan, wife of one of his staff, to turn the song into an advertising jingle for Billy Tea. Her version, published in 1903 with a few more changes to the words and a brisk accompaniment, became the best-known form of the song. A different &quot;Queensland version&quot;, keeping Paterson's words to another tune of unknown origin, is sung by folk singers. The first recording was made in 1926 by John Collinson and Russell Callow.</p><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waltzing-matilda">Waltzing Matilda</a>&quot; has never been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s official anthem, but from 1976 to 1984 it was one of the &quot;national songs&quot; allowed in place of &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot; at civilian functions. In the 1977 plebiscite on a national song it came second with about 28%, behind &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a>&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-keating">Paul Keating</a> later called it Australia's unofficial national song. It is the quick march of the 1st Battalion, Royal Australian Regiment, and the official song of the US 1st Marine Division, which was stationed in Australia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>; it is sung at rugby internationals, the women's national soccer team is called the Matildas after it, and since 2003 its words have been printed microscopically in Australian passports. In 2012 Winton held the first Waltzing Matilda Day on 6 April. The National Film and Sound Archive says it has been recorded more often than any other Australian song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A ballad of a wandering bush worker who takes a sheep, is cornered by the landowner and the police, and chooses death in a waterhole over capture; his ghost is said to linger there. It is told lightly, with dry humour, and has been read both as a whimsical ditty and as a story of the poor against the powerful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oh there once was a swagman camped in the billabong,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Under the shade of a Coolibah tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And he sang as he looked at the old billy boiling,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who’ll come a’waltzing Matilda with me</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who’ll come a’waltzing Matilda my darling</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who’ll come a’waltzing Matilda with me</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Waltzing Matilda and leading a water-bag</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who’ll come a’waltzing Matildta with me</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down came a jumbuck to drink at the billabong,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Up jumped the swagman &amp; grabbed him with glee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And he said as he put him away in the tucker bag</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>“You’ll come a’waltzing Matilda with me”!—</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chorus: “You’ll come” etc</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down came the squatter a’riding his thoroughbred,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down came policemen, one, two and three,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>“Whose is the jumbuck you’ve got in the tucker bag?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You’ll come a’waltzing Matilda with we.”</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chorus: “You’ll come” etc</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But the swagman he up &amp; he jumped in the water-hole</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Drowning himself by the Coolibah tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And his ghost may be heard as it sings by the billabong</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who’ll come a’waltzing Matilda with me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chorus: “Who’ll come” etc</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: A. B. &quot;Banjo&quot; Paterson (1895). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Waltzing_Matilda" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="FqtttbbYfSM" data-title="Waltzing Matilda: Slim Dusty (official channel)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Slim Dusty (official channel)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Slim Dusty. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FqtttbbYfSM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Waltzing Matilda (film, Pat Hanna)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1933</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Once a Jolly Swagman (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1949</span><span class="sg-chain-h">title from the song's opening words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Once a Learned Doctor</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1950s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody, on the Labor Party split</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">On the Beach (film, score by Ernest Gold)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1959</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used throughout the score</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">God Bless Australia (Jack O'Hagan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Rochester Recruiting Sergeant (Peter Coe)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1970</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reworking of &quot;The Bold Fusilier&quot;, sung to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">And the Band Played Waltzing Matilda (Eric Bogle)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">answer song, quotes a fragment at the end</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tom Traubert's Blues (Tom Waits)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">incorporates elements</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Secret Valley (TV theme)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1980</span><span class="sg-chain-h">faster version of the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Slim Dusty's recording broadcast from Space Shuttle Columbia</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">performance</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fantasia on Waltzing Matilda (Harry Sdraulig)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2020</span><span class="sg-chain-h">concert work for Yo-Yo Ma and Kathryn Stott</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Thou Bonnie Wood o Craigielee (Robert Tannahill, poem)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">before 1806</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the poem of the song the tune descends from</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Thou Bonny Wood of Craigie Lee (James Barr)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1818</span><span class="sg-chain-h">original melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Craigielee (Thomas Bulch, quick march)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1891</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune Christina Macpherson remembered</span></li><li><a href="/songs/botany-bay">Botany Bay</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1885</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover</span></li></ul></section>
